Part1: Six Style Rules (each with Before / After example)
Rule 1: Use second‑person "you" to directly address readers for instructions
Before: Click the green New button on the left side to start creating a repository.
After: You click the green  New  button on the left sidebar to create a repository.
 
Rule 2: Mark UI element names with backticks  ` 
Before: Click the Create repository button.
After: Click the  Create repository  button.
 
Rule3: Write complete, grammatically correct phrases; add articles where missing
Before: Add a short description for repository.
After: Add a short description for the repository.
 
Rule4: Convert procedural paragraphs into numbered ordered lists for step‑by‑step tasks
Before: Fill in the repository name in the box, then select Private for repository visibility. Check the box labeled Add a README file.
After:
1. Fill in your repository name. Select  Private  for repository visibility.
2. Check the checkbox labelled  Add a README file .
 
Rule5: Avoid vague positional descriptions; prefer precise terms like sidebar
Before: click the green New button on the left side
After: click the green  New  button on the left sidebar
 
Rule6: Keep each sentence focused on one single action, do not pack multiple separate operations into one sentence
Before: Fill in the repository name in the box, then select Private for repository visibility.
After: Enter your repository name into the input field. Next, select  Private  to set your repository visibility.
 
Part2: Revised full passage 
How to create a private GitHub repository
1. Navigate to the GitHub homepage. Locate and click the green  New  button on the left sidebar to begin repository creation.
2. Enter your repository name inside the name input box. Set repository visibility by choosing the  Private  option.
3. Tick the checkbox marked  Add a README file . A README file stores introductory information for your project.
4. Type a short description for the repository. This description helps others understand your project quickly if you change repo visibility later.
5. Scroll toward the bottom of this page. Click the  Create repository  button to finish the setup.
Once these steps are finished, your new private repository will load automatically in your browser. You can then upload files or edit your README directly on GitHub.
 
Part3: Six explained changes 
1. Change: Added subject  you  and changed "left side" → "left sidebar"
Linked rule: Rule1 & Rule5
Explanation: Using "you" speaks directly to users. "Left sidebar" is a precise UI term instead of vague "left side".
2. Change: Wrapped all button / checkbox labels with backticks, e.g.  New ,  Create repository 
Linked rule: Rule2
Explanation: Backticks visually distinguish clickable UI items from normal text so readers can spot them easily.
3. Change: Fixed missing article:  for repository  →  for the repository 
Linked rule: Rule3
Explanation: Correct grammar removes confusion for non‑native readers.
4. Change: Rewrote the running sentence “Fill in…then select…” into two separate sentences
Linked rule: Rule6
Explanation: One action per sentence reduces cognitive load when people follow instructions.
5. Change: Restructured all actions into numbered list format
Linked rule: Rule4
Explanation: Numbered lists match the sequence users perform operations, making step‑following intuitive.
6. Change: Added brief explanatory sentences about README function and post‑creation behaviour
Linked rule: Rule6
Explanation: Supplement minimal context without mixing multiple actions into one instruction sentence.

